There is a sequence that happens at the cottage that I did not design so much as
discover. Heat. Cold. Stillness. In that order, with water in each step and the
creek present for all of it.

The cedar sauna sits {/* TODO: Will to confirm — exact placement/orientation */} with a west-facing window onto Hull Creek. You can see the dock from in there, and beyond it, depending on the time of day, either a flat pewter water or the beginning of the gold that comes every evening from May onward. {/* TODO: Will to confirm — what is actually visible through the west window: dock? treeline? both? light angle at dusk? */} You heat until you mean it — until sitting still takes a little work — and then you walk out and off the dock into the creek.

{/* TODO: Will to confirm — sauna temperature setting; typical range is 77–93°C / 170–200°F */}

The creek catches you. The water here is brackish and shallow, fed by the Potomac where it broadens toward the bay, and it is cold enough in the morning to stop a thought mid-sentence. Not punishing. Just immediate. Your whole nervous system redials. {/* TODO: Will to confirm — sensory description of dock water temperature by season: shoulder season (April, October) vs. summer; approximate feel, not a number */} You climb back up the dock ladder, find a chair on the screened porch, and sit. That rest — doing nothing on purpose, watching the water settle — is the part that most people don't expect to be the best part. The Finns have always said it is.

## The tradition, and what the science actually says

The hot-cold-rest sequence has a name in contemporary wellness writing — the Nordic Cycle — though in Finland it has never needed one. Finland has roughly three million saunas for a population of five and a half million people. The protocol is old and simple: fifteen to twenty minutes of heat, then immediate cold-water immersion, then a rest period outside. Two or three rounds. The rest is not optional; it is the point.

The science behind it is real, if more modest than the wellness industry suggests. A long-running Finnish cohort study following over two thousand men found that frequent sauna use — four to seven times per week — was associated with meaningfully lower rates of cardiovascular events over a twenty-year period. That association is large and worth knowing about. It is also observational: people who sauna regularly in Finland may also lead healthier lives in other ways, and randomized trials testing sauna directly have shown smaller, sometimes statistically non-significant effects on cardiovascular markers. So: something is probably there, but no one should book a sauna stay instead of seeing a cardiologist.

The cold-water side has its own small literature. A 2023 neuroimaging study with thirty-three adults found that five minutes in cool water produced measurable improvements in mood — the ratio of positive to negative affect nearly doubled. The study's own authors noted that the neural mechanisms "remain largely unknown." What most people report, and what I notice myself after a hard week, is simpler than any mechanism: you get out of the creek and the week is behind you. Whether that is dopamine or the diving reflex or just the shock of cold water and open air, I am not sure it matters.

